1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Mustafa Hassan — Elanco Tick Tracker. A Python/FastAPI backend for cleaning,
filtering and reporting tick-sighting data.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This backend retrieves Elanco’s tick-sighting data, cleans it and exposes
search, regional totals and reports over time.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Python keeps the processing rules straightforward. FastAPI provides the
endpoints and interactive documentation; HTTPX retrieves the source data.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The README includes environment setup, pinned dependencies and the Uvicorn
command used to run the API locally.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Three modules separate the responsibilities: requests in main.py, cleaning and
filtering in data_processing.py, and summaries in reporting.py.

6
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The API connects the processing and reporting modules. Keeping these rules
separate makes them reusable across endpoints and easier to test.

7
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:15,000
load_sightings accepts optional location, start-date and end-date filters. Every
data endpoint uses this helper for the same loading and processing rules.

8
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Each usable record needs a non-empty ID, date and location. The date must parse
successfully before the record can enter a report.

9
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Validation comes before duplicate detection. A set tracks accepted IDs, avoiding
repeated searches through the growing list of records.

10
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The first valid record for each exact ID is kept. Later valid records with the
same ID are counted as duplicates.

11
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Location and date filters run on the cleaned records. Reports therefore use the
same valid, unique sightings as the search endpoint.

12
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:01,000
The unfiltered endpoint returns 1,000 sightings in this source batch. Its
counters make record cleanup visible to the caller.

13
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Each sighting retains its original fields. Here, no invalid records or duplicate
IDs were removed from the downloaded batch.

14
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:20,000
This request combines London with the 2024 date range. The result contains two
matching sightings, while received still describes the full source batch.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Both boundary dates are included. Location matching ignores case and surrounding
spaces; either date limit can also be used on its own.

16
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The source contains cities rather than regions. An explicit lookup maps known
cities to reporting groups; unmapped cities remain counted under Unknown.

17
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The regional endpoint counts the cleaned sightings in each group. Here, the
unfiltered report covers all 1,000 sightings.

18
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Regions are returned alphabetically. The same location and date filters can
narrow this report before the totals are calculated.

19
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The weekly report groups London’s two 2024 sightings into their calendar weeks.
Each period_start identifies the Monday that begins that week.

20
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The sightings fall in the weeks beginning 15 July and 23 September. Each of
these weeks contains one matching sighting.

21
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Changing period to monthly groups the same sightings by calendar month. The
labels now show the first day of July and September.

22
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Trend results are chronological. Only periods containing matching sightings are
included, and monthly is the default when period is omitted.

23
00:03:49,000 --> 00:04:05,000
In the interactive documentation, the start date is set after the end date to
demonstrate how the API rejects an invalid range.

24
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The response is HTTP 422 with a clear explanation: start_date must be on or
before end_date. This check runs before contacting Elanco.

25
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Upstream timeouts return HTTP 504. Network failures, unsuccessful responses,
invalid JSON and an unexpected outer data structure return HTTP 502.

26
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:37,000
The test command runs the processing, API and reporting suites together.
Controlled upstream responses make these checks repeatable.

27
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The checks cover cleanup, filtering, upstream failures, unknown cities and
reporting boundaries, including weeks and months that cross New Year.

28
00:04:48,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The completed run reports 12 tests and OK. Additional cases are exercised
through subtests, including invalid inputs and both trend periods.

29
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:14,000
The current MVP downloads and processes a full batch in memory for every data
request. The README explains the limits of this approach.

30
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Cleaning uses one pass with an ID set, and filtering is linear. Memory grows
with the batch size; reports also sort their output groups.

31
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:38,000
For larger datasets, the plan separates background ingestion from requests,
bounds the ingestion work and stores records with useful indexes.

32
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Filtering and aggregation would move into storage, with paginated sighting lists
and cached reports. Monitoring and bounded retries would support reliability.

33
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
These are proposed improvements. The current implementation remains the working
in-memory backend demonstrated here, with its limitations documented.

34
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,000
The public repository contains the source, tests and README, including setup
instructions, example requests, design choices and the larger-dataset plan.

35
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Elanco Tick Tracker — Mustafa Hassan. Source code, tests and setup instructions
are available in the public GitHub repository.
